  "throughline": "Most philosophers who study morality ask which morals are true: is it right to lie, what do we owe the poor, is pity a virtue. [Nietzsche](/philosophy/thinker/nietzsche) asks a different and more unsettling question, and asks it of the values themselves. Where did they come from? Who invented 'good' and 'evil,' in what condition, with what needs, and what were those people really doing? *On the Genealogy of Morals*, published in 1887 and subtitled *A Polemic*, is his answer, and it is the most systematic book he ever wrote: three connected essays, each a piece of detective work on a value most people treat as eternal and obvious. The First Essay tracks the words 'good,' 'bad,' and 'evil' back to two rival value systems with two different parents, one born of strength and one born of a festering, powerless hatred he keeps in French, *ressentiment*. The Second Essay digs into guilt and the bad conscience, and finds their root not in heaven but in the brutal old law of creditor and debtor, and in what happens to a wild animal's instincts when society cages them and they have nowhere to go but inward. The Third Essay asks why the ideal of self-denial, the ascetic ideal, has ruled human life for so long, and follows it from the priest all the way to modern science, which Nietzsche says is its latest and most cunning disguise. The book ends on one of the most quoted sentences in philosophy, and the reputation that grew up around it, the caricature of a philosopher of cruelty and the master race, is mostly the work of other hands after he could no longer speak.",
