          "p": "Nietzsche names the moment the second morality is born, and he calls it the slave revolt in morals. It is the central event of the First Essay and one of the most provocative ideas in all of philosophy, so it pays to be exact about what it is and is not. It is not a slave uprising in the political sense, not Spartacus, not anyone storming a palace. It is a revolt in *values*, a revolt that takes place entirely in the realm of meaning, and its weapon is not force but a redefinition of 'good.' The masters could never be beaten in the field; they had all the strength. But they could be beaten on the scoreboard, by people who invented a new scoreboard on which strength counts against its holder. That is the revolt: the powerless, unable to win in the world, win instead by inventing a morality in which their own powerlessness is the highest good and their masters' power is the deepest sin."
