          "p": "The engine of the whole thing is one feeling, and Nietzsche keeps it in French on purpose: *ressentiment*. The French word marks it as a technical term, not the everyday English 'resentment,' because the two are not the same. Ordinary resentment is a flash of anger that flares and vents and is gone. *Ressentiment* is what happens to that anger when it *cannot* vent, because the person is too weak to strike back. Blocked from acting on its hatred in the world, the hatred turns inward and festers; it has nowhere to discharge, so it sits and grows venomous and, crucially, becomes *creative*. Denied a real revenge, it manufactures an imaginary one: a whole moral universe in which the hated enemy is finally damned and the suffering self is finally vindicated. That is the line in the epigraph. The slave revolt begins, Samuel renders it, in the very principle of resentment becoming creative, among creatures deprived of the proper outlet of action who find their compensation in an imaginary revenge."
