          "p": "He reconstructs the prehistory, and it is brutal. In the oldest human societies, long before anything we would call morality, there were contracts: a borrower promises to repay a lender, and to make the promise binding pledges something against failure to pay. What did a debtor with nothing pledge? His body. If he could not repay in goods, Nietzsche shows from ancient law codes, the creditor was entitled to repay himself in the debtor's pain, to cut from the body an amount equivalent to the debt, or to inflict suffering in lieu of money. The Roman Twelve Tables, he notes, even legislated how much flesh the creditors might cut, declaring it no crime if they cut too much or too little. The equivalence that made this make sense is the dark heart of the essay: the creditor, denied his money, is granted instead a *sensation of satisfaction*, the pleasure of being permitted to vent his power on someone helpless, the delight, in the French Nietzsche quotes, of doing harm for the pleasure of doing it. Punishment, at the root, was not about correcting the wrongdoer. It was a license to enjoy cruelty, sold as repayment of a debt."
